{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the people were as complainers.</b> \"The people\" means none other than the wicked, and so it says ״מָה אֶעֱשֶׂה לָעָם הַזֶּה״ (Exodus 17), and it says ״הָעָם הַזֶּה הָרָע״ (Jeremiah 13); but when they are worthy they are called \"My people,\" as it says ״שַׁלַּח עַמִּי״ (Exodus 8:16), ״עַמִּי מֶה עָשִׂיתִי לְךָ״ (Micah 6)." "<b>As complainers.</b> מִתְאוֹנְנִים is none other than a term of pretext — seeking a pretext how to separate from following the Omnipresent; and so it says regarding Samson (Judges 14), ״כִּי תֹאֲנָה הוּא מְבַקֵּשׁ״ (Sifrei)." "<b>Evil in the ears of YHWH.</b> A pretext that is evil in the ears of YHWH — they intend it to reach His ears and provoke Him. They said: Woe to us, how we have been endangered on this journey — three days with no rest from the hardship of the way." "<b>And His anger flared.</b> \"I had intended it for your good — that you would enter the land immediately.\"" "<b>At the edge of the camp.</b> Among the outcasts among them, in their lowliness — these are the mixed multitude. Rabbi Shimon ben Menasya says: among the officers among them and the great ones (see Sifrei)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:10:19.622764253Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}